Nick Vargish wrote:
> I find Gehennom boring because it lacks the features I find most
> interesting in the "normal" levels. In Gehennom you never find shops,
> throne rooms, temples, and so forth.

Yes, the only diversity is where these special levels are located
and that they are quite different to the ordinary Gehennom mazes.

Though, OTOH, you never really need shops any more in Gehennom;
everything is identified by then and there are still enough items
lying around.

And the temples and altars existing in Gehennom are more than I
often have got generated in the upper levels. Though static...
(see below).

> Exploration is what makes NH fun
> for me, and there's very little of that in the maze levels. The
> special levels aren't interesting, because I've already seen them a
> dozen times, and they are the same every time.

I wouldn't call the special levels uninteresting - I always take
these as small quests -, but I completely agree that their static
nature is more or less uninteresting.

I very much like the idea of a lava stream. Or a collapsed part of
the maze, which leaves an open area like the bigroom in the midst
of an ordinary maze level. The latter suggestion would allow the
tougher denizens of Gehennom to surround you thus causing a more
challenging fight than having just two adjacent enemies.

Once every three years I suggest the Sewers to be introduced as a
new type of random levels beneath the castle. But the geology of
the Mazes of Menace seem not to allow for such inventions ;-)

> I often just quit once I've assembled a full ascension kit and
> finish the Valley.

Since, in my games, Gehennom does not take as much time as the upper
dungeon does, I never quit; after all I want to succeed in The Planes
which are again, at least, a challenge boost compared to Gehennom.

